sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2014-tumor-03> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In Xenopus laevis embryos, misexpressing the hyperpolarizing potassium channel Kv1.5 in a blastomere distant from the oncogene-injected blastomere reduces the incidence of induced tumor-like structures by about 35% for Xrel3 and about 29% for Gli1, indicating the suppression is not specific to one oncogene.";
    dc:type "experimental-result";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Remote Kv1.5 hyperpolarization suppresses tumor-like structures from two oncogenes";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Chernet BT, Levin M, “Transmembrane voltage potential of somatic cells controls oncogene-mediated tumorigenesis at long-range”, Oncotarget (2014), doi:10.18632/oncotarget.1935; section “RESULTS › Long-range hyperpolarization suppresses the formation of ITLS”. Scope: 16-cell-stage Xenopus laevis embryos co-injected with Xrel3 or Gli1 and Kv1.5 mRNA in separate blastomeres, scored at stage 34"
}
